00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:08.300
When we give up a little bit of privacy in favor of security, we rarely gain that privacy back.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:16.280
In the wake of 9/11 we got the Patriot Act, which is the largest expansion of our financial surveillance regime

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:22.600
since the Bank Secrecy Act in 1970. We don't want to end up looking like mainland China looks.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.460
With lockdowns still in effect across the country Americans are growing restless.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.800
They've taken something and blown it so far out of poroportion, that it's destroying our country.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.960
There's a system that could make reopening possible without increasing the spread of COVID-19.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.840
It's called contact tracing, and involves tracking down every in-person interaction

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:52.360
that infected individuals have had in the preceding days, and then testing, isolating, and repeating the process.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:57.120
Several countries are using phone applications that use Bluetooth or GPS to generate a record

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.160
of who individuals have come into contact with. In Singapore downloading contact tracing apps

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.140
is voluntary. China and Israel use them to enforce mandatory quarantines and isolation.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.820
Voluntary coronavirus tracing and tracking software for iPhones and Androids.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:19.940
In the US Apple and Google have partnered to create a contact tracking app for iPhones and Android devices,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:26.140
but these technologies are raising serious privacy concerns. It's important that these systems are

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:32.460
lawful and voluntary. It's important that these systems minimize, to the greatest extent possible,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:38.400
the collection of personal information. Alan Butler is a lawyer with the Electronic Privacy Information Center,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.060
which has been urging Congress to build privacy protections into any contact tracing system

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:48.900
deployed in the United States. Butler says it's also vital that the system be entirely voluntary.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:55.500
Wouldn't making it voluntary limit the amount of data able to be collected and therefore undermine

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.060
the effectiveness of the system? I think it's likely that a decent percentage of citizens

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:05.820
may decide to install that app because if it's actually useful then they may find it helpful themselves.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.480
But it won't—there will never be universal coverage for this type of system

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.460
I think, and it's and it's never meant to be, right? It's not a silver bullet. Butler says, like in other countries,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:18.320
these apps would be used in conjunction with manual data collection, and he cautions against

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:25.220
systems deploying any apps that use GPS to track phones like those being used in China and Israel.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:31.190
That really changes the relationship between the government and the citizen it's very different to build

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:36.620
a system you know for citizens to
receive alerts that actually serve a

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.060
purpose to them and serve a public
health purpose versus you know a system

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:46.340
of of you know direct government
enforcement of quarantine orders and

